ud in  the process so my number one  recommendation to anybody doing a  migration like this is don't change  anything you don't have to change you  know we well so I'll come back to that  second so I guess the first thing  actually before that times take this for  granted and I almost forgot we put this  in the slide but but the planning part  is actually essential we were in a hurry  but we took a lot of time to evaluate  the move make sure it was the right move  maybe more importantly we locked  ourselves into a conference room in San  Antonio for like a week to think of all  the things that could go wrong and how  we wanted to do this and have a lot of  debates that time upfront even though we  felt a lot of pressure to move fast was  invaluable in the process so don't  shortchange yourself on thinking about  this stuff ahead of time and asking all  the crazy questions which will never be  all the questions you will find but  you'll find that some of those were it  we're really helpful the second thing  and the one that probably rings the  truest for me because we didn't do this  everywhere is change as few things as  possible it's super tempting and  we fell into this in a couple places but  to want to fix all the problems of your  service and your app while you're  migrating you know I don't know I'm in  an old version of ansible I don't wanna  use that anymore I want to get off a  Postgres let's move to BigTable while we  move to Google that's containerize  everything will go from ansible based  VMs and AWS to containerized new  solution in GCP resist that temptation  it makes the migration way more  complicated and I guarantee you it will  take you 3 times longer to move if you  do that then if you move and then make  the change and so my recommendation is  to avoid that so we we move the biggest  part of our infrastructure probably 75%  of our servers and applications in 90  days and the last 20% that we're moving  over has taken us 18 months because it's  gotten much more predictable people are  changing lots of things the refactoring  that they want to do before they move  never quite finishes and it just sort of  drags out because it's much harder to  bracket I think things around that and  that worked for us like I think in  particular had we tried to take our 20  terabyte Postgres database and migrate  to big table while we're going to GCP  the problem is more things are going to  break and it's more confusing what broke  did it break because BigTable didn't  work for our application did it break  because the Google Cloud is different  did it break because our VPNs didn't  work like all of those things just get  more complicated so I think in some ways  it's kind of the antithesis of sort of a  micro-services or maybe a CIO sort of  incremental change approach the more you  bake into the change the more  complicated it's going to be the more  likely you're gonna fail and it's  actually going to be taking more time  another big thing you heard me sort of  talk about this and I'll get into some  details but data gravity is real it  seems like if you're looking at  migrating particularly for something  like AWS to GCP it seems like the two  most similar things on the planet are s3  and GCS so you want it's easy to just  kind of go like oh I got a bunch of data  in s3 I'm just going to copy that data  to GCSE the problem is when when you  start doing things and not everybody  does this on the scale but when you  start talking about moving seven  petabytes of data it turns out there's  there's not an O'Reilly book that is  very good about how to move seven  petabytes of data so we started  by doing some tests we you know I took a  database that was on AWS and I did a  base back up to GCP and I was totally  blown away by how fast the backup was  and thought wow this whole data copying  thing's gonna be easy the problem is one  database backup doesn't simulate moving  two billion objects from GCS that's all  and that was on one bucket I would say  and I can talk to you about why that is  but we had one bucket with a like multi  billions of objects that we were trying  to move over and so we did this testing  we thought the pipes are big because it  turns out like you know the advertisers  and the ads are all sort of going  between those two networks that both  Amazon and Google or set up to do that  but you know it turns out we use the  there's a cloud migration tool that  Google has it's actually quite good so  we just like we flip that on we pointed  at the bucket we started running our  test and we're like we're going to be  here for six months if this happens and  so we sat down with the Google team and  we figured out that you know part of it  is they're sort of serially going  through our key space object by object  doing these lookups moving this data  over and so what we ended up doing to  make a long story a little bit shorter  is that we worked with Google and how  our thing was set up and we ended up  running 32 separate simultaneous  migration jobs by dividing up the key  space we had a pretty distributed key  space we use md5 sums of all of our  images to be the main storing place so  as widely spread and we literally just  took that that key space set divided it  into the 32 groups and then put those  key spaces into 32 separate migration  jobs and flip the switch and 21 days  later seven and a half petabytes of data  was moved so you can do it but just you  have to know that it's probably you know  like what we did doesn't work for  everybody else there's different ways to  approach it I would also say probably  the other interesting story that we had  to move over was the the Postgres  database so we we have a self we have a  series of RDS databases but we also had  these larger databases that were  self-managed Postgres instances and we  thought the simple thing to do simple  thing to do would just be able to create  a read-only replica of our Postgres  database in Google so we we tried to do  that and  one of the problems that we ran into was  that we did everything inside a plan on  private networks we have regulatory  requirements and other sort of security  things that keeps everything without a  public IP so our databases in AWS had no  public IP so it's basically VPN  connected and we just couldn't get  latency throughput to keep up with our  database performance to create a replica  in GCP running across VPNs what we ended  up doing that actually worked quite well  was we took an elastic IP in in AWS  assigned it to the to the database that  we had and ran a replication across the  public network for the two or three  weeks where we were replicating into GCP  and it turns out the public network  latencies weren't as constrained as VPNs  we actually got that to work and and so  we were running for a long time with  with a replica in GCP off of our primary  database in AWS the thing that we missed  was we got so comfortable with that and  I was going to embarrass the guy on the  team who was here because he was going  to show up but he must have known I was  gonna talk about him anyway he got sick  our main DBA got sick we all took our  eyes we actually had a graph that showed  our replication stats we took our eye  off the ball we basically got everything  moved to over and ready to flip the  switch from that being the replica to  being the primary and we had had some  network latency over the weekend before  we did it that caused our other one to  get out of sync and we ended up having  to wait two extra days while the  database caught back up and we we sort  of got that solved and flipped the  switch so we kind of did part of the  part of the migration on a Friday  figured out it was going to work came  back in Monday morning that replicated  caught up we flipped it went the rest of  the way which was good because will was  onstage at next the Tuesday after that  but we got it all done on time so I just  think the whole data thing actually  turned out to be quite hard we think  about things like BigTable and these  sort of systems that are very different  but actually it's the things that look  the same that aren't quite the same that  are more the most complicated parts  about a move like this so for us I think  if you're coming from an AWS to Google  environment the biggest challenges for  us were to understand networking you  know you kind of think networking is  networking TCP is TCP but I will say  this is an area where the two  environments are probably the most diff  and I don't even you know well I don't  know my team will pick one that's better  at worse I don't even know that it's  better or worse they're just different  it's helpful to know what some of those  are probably the biggest thing for us  and this is getting better I mean I  talked a little bit about the need for  the VPNs just how to set those up like  VPNs are set up different in AWS than  they are in GCP once you've kind of  figured out how to pair them and set  them up they work very well we still do  a lot of back-and-forth we have a huge  amount of traffic going between the two  clouds over VPN and that's worked well  for us  Google's use of Sdn is really powerful  and extremely performant but causes some  things you just don't quite expect  sometimes for us it had to do with  things like first of all the internal  load balancer at Google is actually not  a load balancer like you think of it  it's not a it's not like an 88 proxy or  some kind of load balancer instance that  looks like a device it's actually  forwarding rules in the network that  just moves traffic around so they call  it a load balancer but it's really a  combination of front-end and back-end  forwarding rules in the network that  means you can't do things like terminate  SSL at the load balancer internally  because it's really just moving traffic  to your back-end devices all of which  works fine it's just different and that  was hard for us to get used to some of  the internal shared it's called Terra  PPC I keep wanting call it X PN a few  Google's will probably member it as EXPN  before it became public but the shared  PPC xpn stuff was really early for us  it's still kind of early like we didn't  know early on that you couldn't expect  to have the internal load balancer will  not work across the VPN because you know  it's just a device we know networking  devices should be available anywhere but  when you dig in you understand why that  is and we found ways to sort of work  around that and that all works very well  for us I think also private DNS was a  thing that was missing when we moved it  is being tested now we're actively  testing and hoping to move to private  zone resolution inside of Google and so  all these things I think work fine  they're just different and you need to  be aware of them you know as you're sort  of thinking through how you're going to  do things so I guess in summary for us  look our migration went really well  we're running a massive amount of stuff  in Google it's going great we're moving  new stuff over everyday we are going to  maintain a to cloud actually I guess  technically we have some stuff and  sure we'll be an AWS for our regulatory  environment that has to be in gov cloud  but we're you know and but we're also  digging in a lot mentioned heavy use of  BigTable cloud memory store a lot of the  new services that are coming out we're  strip we're trying to migrate it and  sort of embed - even as we grow we are  focused a lot on kubernetes and service  Metro proaches I will say that partly  because of this private networking  mismatch partly because of just some  experience we have in running kubernetes  we run self hosted kubernetes in google  we continue to evaluate GK as an option  but we actually have our own kubernetes  deployment that we that we run and  manage and that has worked well for us  and actually we can configure you back  on a lot of gke capabilities that have  been put into the public use for us to  do things like access underlying I am  roles in our instances and credentialing  and so forth a lot of work with the move  towards analytics to incorporate GPUs  and TP use into our infrastructure both  in the kubernetes side and in other  places and then there's just this  constant effort on our part to balance  cost and speed take advantage of  preemptable VMs you know understand what  the best patterns are for distributing  our imagery and managing egress costs  and so that's what keeps us busy going  forward now that you know we've survived  the the basis of the migration and we're  really sort of excited about that  [Music]

